1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:08,720
Welcome to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,280
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:32,720
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:37,680
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:43,600
Hello, I'm your host, David Bernard.

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:49,920
Today's conversation is shorter than usual and will be featured in RevenueCat's State of Subscription Apps report.

8
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:57,920
Each episode in this series will explore one crucial topic and share actionable insights from top subscription app operators.

9
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:03,280
With me today, Patrick Rills, Chief Product and Technology Officer at Lose It.

10
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:10,880
On the podcast, I talk with Patrick about testing prices from $5 all the way to $120 per year.

11
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:20,400
Why rising customer acquisition costs forced a pricing rethink and how raising the price allows them to discount more aggressively.

12
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:23,520
Hey Patrick, thank you so much for joining me on the podcast today.

13
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:25,520
Hey David, I'm glad to be on.

14
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:29,200
I think I've almost listened to every single episode of Subclub.

15
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:35,760
I think maybe there might be just a handful that I've never listened to, so this is a dream come true for me.

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:36,560
That's amazing.

17
00:01:36,720 --> 00:01:37,360
I didn't even know that.

18
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:38,720
Thanks for saying that.

19
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:43,360
Well, I wanted to have you on because, you know, I've talked about Lose It before, Storied app.

20
00:01:43,840 --> 00:01:44,880
I don't know if you know this.

21
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:50,320
I was actually building a health and wellness app in 2008 when Lose It was published.

22
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:50,720
Oh really?

23
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:54,240
So I've been following Lose It for what is that, 17 years now.

24
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:56,160
We are in our eighteenth year, yeah.

25
00:01:56,480 --> 00:01:57,360
Yeah, exactly.

26
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:03,120
So this year, you all doubled the price, which is a big deal.

27
00:02:03,360 --> 00:02:15,120
And I mean, you know, everyone's doing price testing and you've done a ton of price testing, but for a storied app like Lose It with so much momentum being part of a publicly traded company, doubling your price is a big deal.

28
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:19,920
This isn't the average like an indie app that was charging 10 bucks a year, doubling to 20.

29
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:21,040
This was a big deal.

30
00:02:21,120 --> 00:02:29,360
So I wanted to start with kind of the thoughts behind it and some lessons that folks can take away from why you did it and what you're learning al along the way.

31
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:30,720
Yeah, sure.

32
00:02:31,520 --> 00:02:39,120
We've had the same price since we launched the premium product in 2012, which is actually before I started at Lose It.

33
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:45,120
The original price for the premium product, the base price was always 39,99 per year.

34
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:53,760
Even before we implemented auto renewing subscriptions, the original product in the app was just a consumable in-app purchase.

35
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:57,840
And then we would have to like send out emails to beg people to renew.

36
00:02:58,320 --> 00:03:01,840
Then when auto renewing subscriptions came out, that fundamentally changed our business.

37
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:06,240
But over those years, since we've held the price constant.

38
00:03:06,640 --> 00:03:08,400
A lot has changed in the app economy.

39
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:26,720
Particularly, I'd say the biggest push for this has been the cost of acquisition has gone up, especially since CACs are going up on all channels because of increased competition and there's been some restrictions.

40
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:33,520
Meta recently restricted health and wellness apps how they can advertise on their platform.

41
00:03:34,560 --> 00:03:35,680
It's driven up costs.

42
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:42,640
And so that's been the main driver besides just other things being more expensive than they were in 2012.

43
00:03:43,120 --> 00:03:50,560
And that's kind of been a historical thing for Lusa as well, is that you had depended way more on organic search.

44
00:03:50,720 --> 00:04:08,480
I mean, having been in the App Store eighteen years, you were really highly ranked for, you know, diet app and weight loss and like all these really big keywords, so historically the app did rely more on this organic push, but then in the last few years you have been pushing more into user acquisition, right?

45
00:04:08,880 --> 00:04:09,600
That's right, yeah.

46
00:04:10,080 --> 00:04:19,680
We had built up a moat since we had been in the App Store since November of 2008, and we had built up quite a moat with hundreds of thousands of five star ratings.

47
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:23,520
Five star reviews showing up in the top of the searches and that still happens.

48
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:27,680
But as competition grows, that's going to happen less and less.

49
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:37,200
So we are looking to really ramp up our paid acquisition and we need the economics for that to be a little bit better.

50
00:04:38,320 --> 00:04:46,080
At a $40 price point, it's, you know, much harder than at a $80 price point to get the ROAS that you need.

51
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:47,440
Yeah, absolutely.

52
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:50,400
So I know you've done some price testing in the past.

53
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:56,880
Explain some of the price testing that led up to this kind of the lessons you learned as you tried these other pro prices?

54
00:04:57,600 --> 00:05:01,600
We didn't come about keeping the price at $40 lightly over those years.

55
00:05:02,320 --> 00:05:05,840
We have been testing constantly up and down the demand curve.

56
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:11,840
We've tested everything from over the years from $5 a year to $120 a year.

57
00:05:12,640 --> 00:05:20,880
And usually what you find is that when you raise the price conversions go down and when you lower the price conversions go up, of course.

58
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:23,920
But usually it doesn't net out.

59
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:32,640
Like we felt like at $40 with the sale system that we had, we were at a pretty good equilibrium.

60
00:05:33,280 --> 00:05:39,200
Like we couldn't figure out a pricing structure that could beat it consistently.

61
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:55,280
However, in late 2024, we started price testing again as phones are getting more expensive, our competitors are getting a lot more expensive, and so we decided to test it again, like maybe they're on to something.

62
00:05:55,920 --> 00:05:59,280
And when we tested it for the first time, it broke even.

63
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:03,520
So I would say like the net, the net broke even.

64
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:07,680
That was quite a surprise for us because that had never happened before.

65
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:10,240
And so we tested it.

66
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:20,800
again on iOS and on Android on different cohorts of users, new users, existing users, reactivated users.

67
00:06:21,600 --> 00:06:23,280
We kept getting similar results.

68
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:28,240
We felt pretty confident that raising the price, we were not going to lose any money.

69
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:37,440
If this is really going to unlock paid acquisition and some other things, such as the marginal cost of AI features for us, then why not do it?

70
00:06:37,840 --> 00:06:40,800
It gives our marketing team so much more room to operate.

71
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:47,920
Since you did these experiments way back in 2024 and you didn't fully adopt that new price until 2026.

72
00:06:48,320 --> 00:06:51,120
I imagine you have a lot of data to be able to look at retention.

73
00:06:51,840 --> 00:07:01,280
One of the things about increasing price is that in revenue cat data that we share in the report, we do see higher retention at lower prices.

74
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:03,040
Now, that's not always going to be the case.

75
00:07:03,360 --> 00:07:08,640
You know, sometimes higher priced products do retain better, but just in general, we do see that in the data.

76
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:12,480
And so I do think that's something a lot of apps do face.

77
00:07:13,120 --> 00:07:18,160
And so have you found retention to at least break even as well?

78
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:21,840
Yeah, I'd say our retention is very stable.

79
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:23,600
And I think that's because.

80
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:31,520
Because even though we are getting less new premium users because of the higher price, our free product is amazing.

81
00:07:32,160 --> 00:07:36,640
Since the beginning, we actually operated from 2008 to 2012 without a paid product.

82
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:45,520
So our product has always been a premium model and our free product has always been very useful to people.

83
00:07:46,240 --> 00:07:47,360
We invest a lot in it.

84
00:07:48,160 --> 00:07:50,640
We make sure that the free product is a great experience.

85
00:07:51,280 --> 00:07:53,280
The premium product is just that much better.

86
00:07:54,160 --> 00:08:01,200
I think, you know, if people are more motivated, they can get the premium features and that helps them out.

87
00:08:01,760 --> 00:08:06,480
a lot, but you can do everything you need to do to lose weight in the free product, and we're very proud of that.

88
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:13,680
You've talked about a couple of things being huge unlocks for raising the price, you know, being able to tolerate higher CAC.

89
00:08:13,840 --> 00:08:22,640
You've talked about the AI features where, you know, the incremental cost of serving these users is getting higher as you increase the cost of servicing some of these features.

90
00:08:23,040 --> 00:08:27,440
What other unlocks have you seen from being able to raise the price?

91
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:39,040
Yeah, over the years, we've built quite a life cycle sale mechanism, and it's a homegrown system that we built into the app and refined over the years..

92
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:46,560
So how much of a discount a user gets at a certain point in their journey is completely built into the experience.

93
00:08:47,440 --> 00:09:06,480
And the higher price point allows us to offer more discounts to more users and try to get more area under the curve there because from zero to 40, there's not as much room to do, you know, 50, 25 percent, 75 percent discounts.

94
00:09:06,800 --> 00:09:10,320
But at $80, we can do more steep discounting.

95
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:21,760
And everyone knows about the sale psychology, you know, the bigger the percent percentage, usually the higher the conversion, even in absolute dollars, the price is higher.

96
00:09:22,080 --> 00:09:25,840
How did the product team react to the increase in price?

97
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:38,480
Because I know there can sometimes be a tension between the revenue and monetization optimization from the product people, maybe have a more kind of purity mindset of like, oh, we want to make it a good deal for the users and stuff.

98
00:09:38,720 --> 00:09:43,120
Yeah, the product team was fully on board and they were actually pushing for it.

99
00:09:43,520 --> 00:09:46,000
They did everything they could to enable this.

100
00:09:46,640 --> 00:09:53,040
Because I think the way they look at it is that all of our main competitors are around the $80 price point.

101
00:09:53,520 --> 00:09:59,520
And not to disparage any of our competitors, but we believe our product is just as good as theirs.

102
00:10:00,480 --> 00:10:03,680
However, our price didn't really reflect that.

103
00:10:04,320 --> 00:10:13,280
They felt like they were doing work that was just as valuable to users as our other competitors' work, but it wasn't being reflected in the price.

104
00:10:13,440 --> 00:10:17,360
And what are prices if not information about how something valuable is?

105
00:10:18,240 --> 00:10:31,280
I was just talking to somebody about pricing and how in the app space, it's not like a shelf at a grocery store where people are looking at the like Campbell's soup and then the store brand soup and doing the math and like picking based on price.

106
00:10:31,360 --> 00:10:36,480
You know, people are coming in from ads and so they don't necessarily have that like frame of reference anyway.

107
00:10:36,960 --> 00:10:53,440
And so even when you think about a lower price being a differentiator, it's not necessarily because people aren't necessarily price shopping in that same way, especially once you're pushing deeper into paid user acquisition, you're probably getting less of that price comparison anyway.

108
00:10:53,840 --> 00:10:54,240
That's true.

109
00:10:54,880 --> 00:11:03,120
I will say that with the caveat of that, our lower price points did help us, I think, during the early stages of the pandemic.

110
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:03,840
Right.

111
00:11:04,320 --> 00:11:05,360
When people were.

112
00:11:05,440 --> 00:11:14,480
were trapped in their houses unsure how they were going to meet their health and fitness goals, but also unsure of their financial situation.

113
00:11:15,040 --> 00:11:24,080
Because I know we don't like to go back and remember what it was like in March and April of 2020, but we had no idea what was going to happen.

114
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:28,800
And so I think being a low cost option really benefited us then.

115
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:31,920
But since, I think you're absolutely right.

116
00:11:32,240 --> 00:11:39,280
Like we haven't seen any, I don't think there's too much comparison shopping going on either, which is what another reason why this is working.

117
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:47,920
I'm wondering if you did consider a higher tier of price versus raising the price for everybody and why you decided against doing that?

118
00:11:48,640 --> 00:11:50,160
We're considering all options.

119
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:53,600
Higher tier is not off the table.

120
00:11:54,160 --> 00:11:59,360
We've since implemented, you know, ad based monetization in the free product.

121
00:12:00,080 --> 00:12:02,720
We're looking to monetize in all sorts of ways.

122
00:12:03,280 --> 00:12:06,560
We've added in-app purchases, one-off in-app purchases in the app.

123
00:12:06,960 --> 00:12:10,960
We've added partnerships in the app, you know, affiliate revenue.

124
00:12:11,200 --> 00:12:12,560
So nothing's off the table.

125
00:12:12,640 --> 00:12:14,960
I would say we have not decided against it.

126
00:12:15,840 --> 00:12:18,160
We're always open to new ideas to monetize.

127
00:12:18,560 --> 00:12:19,840
But the decision was to raise the.

128
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:29,920
the floor, raise the main primary subscription price, and that still leaves the opportunity to add tiers on top and explore other monetization.

129
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:31,520
Exactly, yeah.

130
00:12:31,840 --> 00:12:34,720
We have such a loyal user base.

131
00:12:35,840 --> 00:12:45,120
We love our users and they love us, and there's a subset of users who want the very best Luzit has to offer.

132
00:12:45,920 --> 00:12:55,680
If we work really hard and create a product that generates a lot of value, we have a lot of great users who are totally on board with that.

133
00:12:56,000 --> 00:12:56,560
That's awesome.

134
00:12:56,960 --> 00:13:02,800
And one thing you didn't do was raise prices on existing users, which is always such a tough decision.

135
00:13:03,040 --> 00:13:04,000
I've heard it go both ways.

136
00:13:04,240 --> 00:13:08,880
I've heard some people say they doubled prices and it was just and they didn't grandfather users.

137
00:13:09,280 --> 00:13:20,160
And because their customer base was so loyal, they just made way more money and they saw some churn, but it was significantly offset by the increase of the price.

138
00:13:20,640 --> 00:13:22,080
Why did you choose not to?

139
00:13:22,960 --> 00:13:25,040
Yeah, there were a few reasons why.

140
00:13:25,680 --> 00:13:36,480
So the way that you have to increase the price on existing subscriptions in the App Store, I feel like the UX is confusing and cumbersome.

141
00:13:37,040 --> 00:13:47,600
I think you get like a notification that says when you renew, you know, the price is going to go up and then they have to go to the app store and either opt in or not opt in.

142
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:50,800
I didn't feel like that was a good experience for our users.

143
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:53,200
For us, it was impossible to AB test.

144
00:13:53,600 --> 00:13:53,680
Yeah.

145
00:13:54,160 --> 00:13:57,760
It was a huge risk for us that we felt like we couldn't take on.

146
00:13:58,240 --> 00:14:02,480
Like what is the attrition rate when you raise the price of an existing subscription?

147
00:14:02,720 --> 00:14:03,120
I don't know.

148
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:05,760
That might be in the state of the subscription apps report.

149
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:06,400
I don't know.

150
00:14:06,480 --> 00:14:08,480
But to us, it's like we couldn't risk it.

151
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:09,760
Like, is it 75 percent?

152
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:11,040
Is it 25 percent?.

153
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:13,360
It was just too big of an unknown for us.

154
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:23,200
And also, we've always had a history of whenever we've made policy or product changes over the years to grandfather in our loyal users.

155
00:14:23,520 --> 00:14:37,200
When we were at the App Growth Annual in October, our growth person and one of our product managers, Danielle and Burton, presented on how we moved the Parcode scanner from behind the paywall.

156
00:14:37,680 --> 00:14:41,120
When we actually made that change, we grandfathered in free users to that change.

157
00:14:41,360 --> 00:14:44,080
And that change was only for new users as well.

158
00:14:44,880 --> 00:14:58,080
We've always had just a tradition of doing that just because, again, we love our users and we feel like whatever bargain they entered into whenever they came to us, we try to keep as much as possible.

159
00:14:58,640 --> 00:14:58,720
Yeah.

160
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:00,320
I mean, there is that incentive.

161
00:15:00,640 --> 00:15:02,400
I've talked about this on the podcast before.

162
00:15:02,960 --> 00:15:09,680
I got into some special deal with Headspace where it was, I think, I was paying like three dollars a month or something.

163
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:14,160
And then at that time the price had gone up to twelve dollars a month or sixty dollars a year.

164
00:15:14,960 --> 00:15:19,760
And I knew if I canceled when I resubscribed, I would have to pay a lot more.

165
00:15:20,400 --> 00:15:24,640
And so I stayed with that subscription even though I wasn't even using the app much at the time because.

166
00:15:24,880 --> 00:15:26,720
because there was that incentive to stay around.

167
00:15:26,880 --> 00:15:29,840
So I think there is a little bit of that too with your grandfathered users.

168
00:15:30,240 --> 00:15:36,480
And then I would assume that if they do churn and come back to the app, then they're going to see that new price and have to pay that higher price, right?

169
00:15:36,800 --> 00:15:36,960
Right.

170
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:40,480
So I think that's an interesting experiment we can run right off the bat, right?

171
00:15:40,800 --> 00:15:48,640
Like if someone has marked a cheaper subscription to not renew, you know, we can have an intervention there that is like, are you sure?

172
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:50,640
Because it's going to be different.

173
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:54,960
You've had about a month of rolling out this new price to everyone.

174
00:15:55,280 --> 00:15:58,800
And it was like the most important month for a health and fitness app.

175
00:15:59,040 --> 00:16:04,400
So what are the early signs of having rolled this out across the entire user base?

176
00:16:05,200 --> 00:16:11,680
I would say, so far, the results have been exactly our experiment results, which hardly ever happens, right?

177
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:25,600
Like usually, usually when you release something out in the wild, it's like a little bit different, but so far it has held up to our experiment results and that is a credit to our growth marketing team and our product team.

178
00:16:25,920 --> 00:16:29,680
They really tested this rigorously, really analyzed the data.

179
00:16:30,560 --> 00:16:33,920
And so, so far, it's been holding up very, very well.

180
00:16:34,560 --> 00:16:35,680
We're very excited about it.

181
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:36,320
Awesome.

182
00:16:36,800 --> 00:16:38,640
Well, I think it's a great place to wrap up.

183
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:40,880
Anything you wanted to share as we wrap up?

184
00:16:41,280 --> 00:16:48,400
I'll put a link to Lose It and your LinkedIn in the show notes, but anything else you wanted to shout out as we wrap up?

185
00:16:48,800 --> 00:16:50,960
Yeah, we need iOS developers.

186
00:16:51,280 --> 00:16:57,360
So if you are an iOS developer, please, please seek me out on LinkedIn.

187
00:16:57,680 --> 00:16:58,320
We're trying to grow.

188
00:16:58,560 --> 00:16:59,760
We're trying to do a lot of things.

189
00:17:00,400 --> 00:17:00,880
We need people.

190
00:17:01,360 --> 00:17:02,560
Yeah, it's a really cool team.

191
00:17:02,720 --> 00:17:06,640
I've met a lot of folks behind Lose It and they like everyone I've talked to.

192
00:17:06,880 --> 00:17:08,480
So yeah, it's been great.

193
00:17:08,720 --> 00:17:14,080
You know, the app growth annual has been a really fun place to to to meet folks from the industry.

194
00:17:14,240 --> 00:17:20,160
And so over I think I've met six or eight people now from the Lose It team between App Growth Annual and other events and stuff.

195
00:17:20,320 --> 00:17:21,040
So great team.

196
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:23,200
If you are an IOS developer, I know a good one.

197
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:25,440
I can say it's good people.

198
00:17:25,680 --> 00:17:26,640
Good people and a cool product.

199
00:17:27,120 --> 00:17:28,080
Well, thank you very much.

200
00:17:31,040 --> 00:17:32,320
Thanks so much for listening.

201
00:17:33,280 --> 00:17:36,720
If you have a minute, please leave a review in your favorite podcast player.

202
00:17:37,520 --> 00:17:42,400
You can also stop by chat dot subclub dot com to join our private community.
